---
const SIGNALS = [
  {
    signal: "matched nothing",
    means: "The node is gone, or it was renamed.",
  },
  {
    signal: "value failed its declared type",
    means:
      "The selector still matches something. It is now grabbing the wrong node.",
  },
];
---

<section class="section overflow-hidden" id="invalidation">
  <div class="mesh mesh-paid"></div>

  <div class="shell relative z-10">
    <h2 class="h-section" data-reveal>There is no TTL on a selector.</h2>

    <p class="lede mt-6" data-reveal>
      A selector that still works is still correct. Expiring it on a timer only
      buys LLM calls.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
      {
        SIGNALS.map((s) => (
          <div
            class="min-w-0 rounded-card border border-rule bg-base-2 px-5 py-5"
            data-reveal
          >
            <p class="mb-2 font-mono text-micro text-paid">{s.signal}</p>
            <p class="text-copy text-fg-dim">{s.means}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <p class="copy mt-10" data-reveal>
      Those are the only two signals that drop a cached selector, and they are
      the same event seen from two angles: the page moved. When one fires, that
      one field is re-derived. Nothing else on the page is touched, and the
      cache repairs itself without anyone noticing first.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
